# Cloudflare Pages headers
# Critical: without these, .md files download instead of rendering in the browser,
# and some crawlers skip them entirely because the content type is wrong.
#
# This file is the standby. The live host is Hostinger, which reads .htaccess and
# ignores this. The two must stay in agreement, or moving hosts silently changes
# behavior with nothing failing loudly to warn you. If you edit a header in one,
# edit it in the other. The one thing this file cannot express is the deny list:
# Cloudflare Pages has no equivalent of "Require all denied", so on that host the
# protection is entirely "do not upload the build scripts".
#
# THE SET IS THREE, NOT TWO. vercel.json is this same policy a third time, for
# Vercel, and .htaccess names it as well. A header added to any one of them
# belongs in all three. That is not hypothetical: on 2026-08-13 vercel.json was
# found missing Strict-Transport-Security, X-Frame-Options and the
# frame-ancestors CSP that the other two carry, and it was brought back into
# line. Drift here is invisible by construction, because two of the three do
# nothing until the day someone moves hosts, which is the day nobody is looking.
#
# Splats span slashes, so /*.md also covers /writing/*.md. Verify after any move:
#   curl -sI https://danieloon.ai/writing/agentic-payments.md

/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: inline
  X-Robots-Tag: all
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/*.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: inline
  X-Robots-Tag: all
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/sitemap.xml
  Content-Type: application/xml; charset=utf-8

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
